The code statements that lead to the bug are:
destroy_workqueue(ndev->cmd_wq);
...
nfc_unregister_device(ndev->nfc_dev);
Reason:
In nci_unregister_device(), the workqueue cmd_wq is destroyed before the device is unregistered from rfkill. This allows a race where rfkill can still trigger nci_close_device() after cmd_wq is invalid, causing lockdep warnings or use-after-free when flushing the destroyed workqueue.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
nfc_unregister_rfkill(ndev->nfc_dev);
...
nfc_remove_device(ndev->nfc_dev);
Reason:
By calling nfc_unregister_rfkill() before nci_close_device() and workqueue destruction, rfkill can no longer invoke nci_close_device() after the workqueue is destroyed. Replacing nfc_unregister_device() with nfc_remove_device() ensures rfkill removal is done early while still cleaning up the device later in the correct order.

